# People HRMS — project root environment reference
#
# This repository has two workspaces, each with its own environment:
#   app/api/   — FastAPI backend
#   app/web/   — Nuxt frontend
#
# Each workspace already has its own .env.example (the canonical reference) and
# .env.local (local overrides, never committed). This top-level file is a short
# pointer so a developer can find the right file quickly, plus the non-negotiable
# ground rules for secrets across environments.
#
# ── Quick start (local development)
#
#   1. PostgreSQL: create a local database, e.g. hrms_db, owned by hrms_user.
#      The reference credentials are documented in app/api/.env.example.
#   2. API:
#        cp app/api/.env.example app/api/.env.local
#        # Edit app/api/.env.local:
#        #   DATABASE_URL     — your local PostgreSQL URL
#        #   JWT_SECRET       — at least 32 random characters
#        #   SUPER_ADMIN_*    — strong, unique bootstrap credentials
#        #   MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY — base64-encoded 32 random bytes
#        #   ERP_API_CLIENTS  — fill before connecting a real ERP
#      Start: cd app/api && uv sync && uv run fastapi dev main.py
#   3. Web:
#        cp app/web/.env.example app/web/.env.local
#      Start: cd app/web && pnpm install && pnpm dev
#
# ── Environments
#
#   LOCAL DEVELOPMENT  — ENVIRONMENT=development
#     - Weak dev defaults are accepted by the startup validator.
#     - .env.local is the right place for local overrides.
#     - BOOTSTRAP_SEED_DATA=true is acceptable for the first local start.
#
#   TESTING  — ENVIRONMENT=test
#     - Use a dedicated test database ( conventionally named *_test).
#     - TESTING does NOT imply production-safe secrets — still use strong values
#       for JWT_SECRET, SUPER_ADMIN_PASSWORD, MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY, ERP_API_CLIENTS.
#     - Set BOOTSTRAP_SEED_DATA=true only when you explicitly want seed data in
#       the test DB (integration tests may prefer a known state).
#
#   PRODUCTION  — ENVIRONMENT=production
#     - Weak dev defaults are REJECTED at startup.
#     - Every secret must be replaced with a strong, unique value.
#     - BOOTSTRAP_SEED_DATA=true ONLY for the very first production start,
#       then set it back to false immediately.
#     - Use a secret manager / deployment secrets for real credentials.
#     - AUTH_THROTTLE_BACKEND=redis is REQUIRED for more than one worker.
#
# ── Secrets: ground rules
#
#   - .env, .env.local, .env.production, .env.staging, etc. are NEVER committed.
#     They are covered by .gitignore.
#   - .env.example files ARE committed and contain placeholders only.
#   - Real secrets live in:
#       - local:  .env.local (developer machine only)
#       - CI:     CI secrets / environment variables (not in the repo)
#       - prod:  deployment secret store (platform secrets, vault, etc.)
#   - Never paste real passwords, tokens, HMAC secrets, encryption keys, or
#     database URLs into chat, issue trackers, or docs.
#
# ── Workspace env files
#
#   Backend configuration reference:
#     app/api/.env.example   — full variable reference with defaults and notes
#     app/api/.env.local     — local overrides (gitignored)
#
#   Frontend configuration reference:
#     app/web/.env.example   — NUXT_HRMS_API_BASE and allowed origins
#     app/web/.env.local     — local overrides (gitignored)
#
#   This top-level .env.example is intentionally minimal: the workspace files
#   are the authority for variable names, defaults, and production requirements.
